+A mark the player takes on their own sheet waits five seconds for its Undo, and a mark an open soak or Death Defy offer holds waits for the answer; either is drawn in its box half inked with a dashed oxblood edge, never filled (a held mark on the Lightkeeper's read-only copy too, with its "held for" line, since fix round 6), until the sheet with it on comes back from the table, and screen readers hear "1 held" with the track's count (playtest, 2026-10-10: the box went empty for the offer's 30 seconds). It stays so while it is on its way too: sent when the Undo runs out, let go or used on its offer, each mark of a harm until its own sheet comes, so the box never shows empty for the time the table takes to answer. Under the record the Undo strip says when the mark goes ("Bleed mark: it goes to the table when the Undo runs out."), never that it was taken, and a dashed line names the offer that holds one ("Bleed mark held for Death Defy."). The strip's Undo and the offer card's Use and Take the mark are 44px on touch screens. On a wide record (a mouse, 38rem and up) that line and the strip share one row in smaller type, so with both up the whole record still fits the 1440x900 desk (stacked, they ran 62px past the sheet's foot); where a note still runs the record past the foot of a column that scrolls inside itself (a long ability text, a 1366x768 laptop), the column brings the record's foot into view, its top kept in view. Below xl the offer card is fixed over the foot of the page, so the page keeps room the card's height under the desk while it is up, and the record (the held box and its line) scrolls clear above the card (on a phone nothing could be scrolled out from under it). An answer the table refuses ("In the Trenches cannot soak this mark: ...", after which the mark lands) is said in oxblood under the marks until the next mark is taken or a new soak offer comes (the Death Defy offer that may follow keeps it up); a drive point no longer waiting holds no mark and